@@ -0,0 +1,116 @@
+# Small Go files
+
+**The standard.** Target Go files under 600 lines. A *violation* is a non-test `.go` file anywhere
+in the repository with more than **1,000 lines** (every line of the file counts, blank and comment
+lines included). 600 is the target and is not enforced. Test files (`*_test.go`) are exempt.
+Test helpers that are not `_test.go` files (for example `tests/check/*.go`,
+`pkg/storage/test/*.go`) and generated code (`internal/mocks/`) are measured.
+
+**The ratchet.** Existing violations are recorded in `.seikika/results.json`. A pull request fails when:
+- a file already over 1,000 lines grows, by even one line;
+- a file at or under 1,000 lines crosses 1,000;
+- a new (or renamed) file arrives with more than 1,000 lines.
+
+Shrinking a violating file, or bringing it to 1,000 or below, passes and is reported as an
+improvement, so the baseline can be tightened. Edits to files under the limit, and to non-Go
+files, pass unchanged.
+
+All commands run from the repository root and need [Seikika](https://github.com/seikika/seikika)
+0.1.1 (`seikika` and `seikika-connector-runner` on `PATH`), `python3`, `git` and `jq`.
+
+## Report: every violating file, as JSON
+
+```bash
+seikika report .seikika -o json | jq '[.scores["small-go-files"] | to_entries[] | select(.value.rules["at-most-1000-lines"] == "fail") | {file: (.key | ltrimstr("go-file:")), lines: (.value.evidence["at-most-1000-lines"][0] | split(" ")[0] | tonumber)}]'
+```
+
+Prints `[{"file": "cmd/run/run.go", "lines": 1270}, …]`: 15 files on 2026-11-21. For the
+human-readable version (every file with its line count and the fix), run `seikika report .seikika`.
+
+## Gate: fail when the ratchet breaks
+
+```bash
+seikika report .seikika --baseline .seikika/results.json --out /dev/null
+```
+
+Exit 0 when nothing got worse, 1 on a regression, 2 when Seikika can't run. It prints one line per
+change, for example:
+
+```
+✗ worse   small-go-files · go-file:cmd/run/run.go: at-most-1000-lines evidence grew 271 → 272
+✗ worse   small-go-files · go-file:pkg/server/check.go: at-most-1000-lines now fails
+✗ worse   small-go-files · go-file:pkg/server/huge.go: new, at none; new entities must reach bronze
+✓ better  small-go-files · go-file:cmd/run/run.go: at-most-1000-lines evidence shrank 271 → 270
+```
+
+The evidence count is the number of lines over 1,000, plus one. Add `--verdict verdict.json` for a
+machine-readable verdict (`regressions`, `improvements`, `changes`). Add `--ci` to also fail when
+`results.json` is out of date (unrecorded improvements or new files).
+
+CI: [`.github/workflows/seikika-standards.yml`](../.github/workflows/seikika-standards.yml) runs
+the same gate (`mode: check`) on every pull request, through Seikika's `actions/report` action. The
+job summary lists regressions and improvements.
+
+## Campaign: progress toward zero violating files
+
+```bash
+seikika report .seikika -o web --out "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/openfga-standards" > /dev/null && jq '.campaigns["small-go-files-zero"] | {campaign: .title, status, baseline: .inScope, current: (.inScope - .meeting), percentDone: .percent, percentExact: (if .inScope > 0 then (.meeting * 1000 / .inScope | floor) / 10 else 0 end), targetPercent}' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/openfga-standards/report.json"
+```
+
+`baseline` is the number of files that violated when the campaign started (15 on 2026-11-21),
+`current` how many of them still do, and `percentDone` the share fixed (Seikika rounds down;
+`percentExact` has one decimal). The campaign's scope is frozen in
+[`campaign.yaml`](./campaign.yaml), so it measures paying down the old violations; the ratchet stops
+new ones. The HTML report (`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/openfga-standards/index.html`) shows the same progress
+in its Campaigns tab.
+
+If a file in the campaign is deleted or renamed, it leaves the campaign's scope (the baseline count
+drops by one) instead of counting as done. When you split a file, keep the original file and move
+code into new files in the same package, so the path stays in the campaign.
+
+## Update the baseline
+
+After a change that shrinks or fixes files (the gate printed `✓ better`), or adds new files:
+
+```bash
+seikika report .seikika --baseline .seikika/results.json --update --out /dev/null
+git add .seikika/results.json
+```
+
+`--update` refuses to write while anything regressed, so it can only tighten the bar. Never hand-edit
+or hand-merge `results.json`: on a merge conflict, take either side and rerun `--update` on the merged
+tree.
+
+Renaming or moving a file over 1,000 lines is a regression: to the ratchet it is a new file over the
+limit. Split it below 1,000 in the same PR, or, if a maintainer approves, reset the baseline: delete
+`.seikika/results.json`, rerun `--update`, and review the diff (it must show only the renamed
+path). Seikika has no way to accept one named regression.
+
+`internal/mocks/mock_storage.go` is generated by `make generate-mocks`: growing the
+`OpenFGADatastore` interface grows it and fails the gate. If that happens, exempt it (below)
+rather than hand-editing the mock.
+
+## Change the standard
+
+Everything lives in [`seikika.yaml`](./seikika.yaml) (the `small-go-files` scorecard) and
+[`campaign.yaml`](./campaign.yaml). Check your change with `seikika validate .seikika`, then run the
+Gate and update the baseline.
+
+| Change | Where |
+|---|---|
+| **Threshold** (e.g. 1000 → 800) | In `seikika.yaml`, replace `1000` in the rule's name (`at-most-1000-lines`), `check`, `evidence` and `fix`; the same in `campaign.yaml`, and in the Report command above. A lower threshold makes passing files fail (and `--update` refuses), so reset the baseline (delete `.seikika/results.json`, rerun `--update`, review the diff), then decide the campaign's scope |
+| **Exempt a file** | Add its path to the list at the end of `appliesTo` in `seikika.yaml`: `entity.path in ['internal/mocks/mock_storage.go']`. The gate then reports it as `✓ better … now passes`; run `--update` |
+| **Exempt a pattern** | Add a condition to `appliesTo`, e.g. `entity.path.startsWith('internal/mocks/')` |
+| **Which files are measured** | `.seikika/go-file-lines.py` (every tracked or untracked, non-ignored `**/*.go`). It only measures; the rule and exemptions stay in the manifest |
+| **Campaign scope or dates** | `campaign.yaml`: the frozen file list, `start`, optional `end`, `targetPercent` and `milestones` |
+
+## How it works
+
+| File | Role |
+|---|---|
+| `seikika.yaml` | The repo portal: a `runner` connector, the `GoFile` kind, the `small-go-files` scorecard and its ratchet floor (`newEntitiesAtLeast: bronze`) |
+| `go-file-lines.py` | Emits one record per Go file: path, line count, and line numbers (so each line over 1,000 is one evidence item, and the ratchet sees growth of one line) |
+| `campaign.yaml` | The campaign and its scorecard over the 15 files that violated the 1,000-line limit on 2026-11-21 |
+| `results.json` | The committed baseline (generated; do not edit) |
+
+Adapted from the Codex team's Rust module-size standard (`.seikika/` in that repository).
